      'Tools of the Federal Reserve',
      'Tools of the Federal Reserve The tools of the Federal Reserve are:\n\n• Setting the discount rate: The discount rate is the rate that the Federal Reserve charges member banks to borrow reserves. To loosen credit, the Fed lowers the borrow reserves. To tighten credit, the Fed increases the discount rate. To loosen credit, the Fed lowers the rate.\n\nDiscount rate changes are made periodically in response to economic activity levels.\n\nTest-Taking Tip: The discount rate is the only rate set directly by the Federal Reserve.\n\n• Open market operations: The Federal Reserve trading desk in New York adjusts credit availability daily through repurchase (repo) and reverse-repurchase (reverse repo) agreements with primary government dealers.\n\nThis is an overnight repurchase agreement, which loosens overnight credit by injecting more cash into the system.\n\n• Overnight repo loosens credit: Under a repo, the Fed buys Treasury securities from the dealer, with an agreement to sell them back the next day. This is an overnight repurchase agreement, which loosens\n\n This is an overnight reverse repurchase agreement, which tightens overnight credit by taking money out of the economy.\n\n• Overnight reverse repo tightens credit: Under a reverse-repo, the Fed gives the dealer a temporary pledge of government securities in return for cash. It does this by selling Treasury securities to the dealer, with an agreement to buy them back the next day. This is an overnight reverse repurchase agreement,\n\nTest-Taking Tip: The most commonly used tool of the Fed is open market operations.\n\n• Influencing the federal funds rate by open market operations: When the Fed uses open market operations, the interest rate that is affected is the federal funds rate, which is the interest rate commercial banks charge each other for making overnight loans of reserves from bank to bank.\n\nTest-Taking Tip: The federal funds rate is a market rate that is not set by the Federal Reserve.\n\n• Setting the reserve requirements: The Fed sets the level of reserves that member banks must maintain. Increasing the reserve requirement would tighten credit, while reducing the reserve requirement would loosen credit. This tool is rarely used.\n\n• Setting margin requirements: The Fed sets initial margin rates for nonexempt securities under Regulation T. To tighten credit, the Fed can increase margins; to loosen credit, margins can be lowered. The Fed has not changed margin requirements since 1969.\n\n• Quantitative easing: The Fed can enter the market to buy up government, municipal, or corporate bonds. In doing so, the Fed injects money into the system and increases the money supply. If the economy is overheating, the Fed can sell the bonds it holds and decrease the money supply.\n\nFederal Reserve actions affect the major interest rates that are used in the economy. These interest rates are (from lowest to highest):\n\n• Federal funds rate: The rate of interest that member banks charge each other for overnight loans of reserves. This is a market rate that is not directly set by the Fed but is indirectly influenced by the Fed\'s open market operations.\n• Discount rate: The rate at which member banks borrow reserves directly from the Federal Reserve. This is the only rate set directly by the Fed. Banks use this discount rate if they are unable to borrow from other banks. The Fed usually sets this slightly higher than the federal funds rate because they only want banks to use it as a last resort.\n\n• Broker call rate: The rate banks charge broker-dealers to finance customers\' margin trading.\n\nPrime rate: The rate at which banks will make unsecured loans to their best commercial customers.\n\nTo summarize, the interest rates, from lowest to highest, are: Federal funds rate Discount rate Broker call rate Prime rate',
